WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:04.435
I purchased a microscope near Qasr El Einy in Cairo.

00:00:04.435 --> 00:00:04.584
It

00:00:04.584 --> 00:00:08.105
was not laboratory equipment assigned to me by a

00:00:08.105 --> 00:00:08.912
university.

00:00:15.633 --> 00:00:18.678
Bringing it with me to Qatar was a considerable

00:00:18.678 --> 00:00:19.131
hassle.

00:00:19.131 --> 00:00:22.310
The difficulty did not end when I had paid for it.

00:00:27.925 --> 00:00:31.339
With a microscope at home, observation no longer

00:00:31.339 --> 00:00:31.908
depended

00:00:31.908 --> 00:00:35.472
entirely on laboratory booking hours or a scheduled

00:00:35.472 --> 00:00:35.892
class.

00:00:42.342 --> 00:00:45.415
The instrument let me pursue small independent

00:00:45.415 --> 00:00:48.756
observations simply because I wanted to understand

00:00:48.756 --> 00:00:49.424
something.

00:00:55.217 --> 00:00:59.047
Ownership did not confer a scientific result; it made

00:00:59.047 --> 00:01:02.949
curiosity more practical by providing repeated access.

00:01:09.217 --> 00:01:12.363
Years later, across developmental biology and

00:01:12.363 --> 00:01:12.992
materials

00:01:12.992 --> 00:01:16.631
science, I still recognise the value of being able to

00:01:16.631 --> 00:01:17.729
look for myself.
